---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import BenchmarkComparison from "../../components/BenchmarkComparison";
import { BENCHMARK_METRICS, COMPARED, LATENCY_PASS, SYSTEM_SPECS, formatMetric } from "../../data/benchmarks";

const compared = BENCHMARK_METRICS.filter((m) => m.githubDesktop !== null);
const ratio = (a: number, b: number) => `${(b / a).toFixed(b / a >= 10 ? 0 : 1)}×`;
---

<DocsLayout
  currentSlug="benchmarks"
  title="Performance"
  description="Corvene against GitHub Desktop and GitKraken on one machine, the budgets it holds itself to, and how to reproduce every number."
>
  <p>
    Every number here was measured on {COMPARED.date}, on an {SYSTEM_SPECS.machine}: Corvene's release build against
    {COMPARED.githubDesktop} and {COMPARED.gitKraken}, launch and memory on each app's own repository, interaction
    latencies on the same large fixture repository ({SYSTEM_SPECS.fixture}).
  </p>

  <h2 id="comparison">Against GitHub Desktop and GitKraken</h2>
  <p>
    <code>tools/perf/apps.py</code> starts each application's executable directly and measures from the outside, the same
    way for all three: the time until its first window is on screen (CGWindowList), the Electron apps' first contentful
    paint over the DevTools protocol (within 60 ms of their window every time), and after a minute idle the physical memory
    footprint of the process and its helpers (Activity Monitor's Memory column). Five rounds, the apps taking turns so each
    met the same machine conditions; the 1-minute load average sat between 9 and 15 throughout, other builds being
    under way, so the absolute times are a little pessimistic for all three. Each app opened the repository it had open
    last.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Metric</th><th>Corvene</th><th>{COMPARED.githubDesktop}</th><th>{COMPARED.gitKraken}</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {compared.map((m) => (
        <tr>
          <td>{m.name}</td>
          <td><strong>{formatMetric(m.corvene, m.unit)}</strong></td>
          <td>{formatMetric(m.githubDesktop!, m.unit)} <span class="text-fg-subtle">({ratio(m.corvene, m.githubDesktop!)})</span></td>
          <td>
            {m.gitKraken === null ? (
              <span class="text-fg-subtle">no keyboard navigation</span>
            ) : (
              <>{formatMetric(m.gitKraken, m.unit)} <span class="text-fg-subtle">({ratio(m.corvene, m.gitKraken)})</span></>
            )}
          </td>
        </tr>
      ))}
      <tr>
        <td>Processes while idle</td><td><strong>1</strong></td><td>4</td><td>7</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Resident set size (RSS), for reference</td><td><strong>109 MB</strong></td><td>486 MB</td><td>1,026 MB</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Medians of five. Fastest single launches: Corvene 231 ms, GitHub Desktop 1,782 ms, GitKraken 2,958 ms. RSS counts
    shared framework pages once per process, which is why it overstates the Electron apps; the footprint is the fair
    column.
  </p>
  <h3 id="interaction">Interaction latency</h3>
  <p>
    The two interaction rows above come from the same actions on the same repository. Corvene is driven over its control
    socket by <code>tools/perf/bench.py</code>, which times each input until the frame that draws the state it asked for.
    GitHub Desktop and GitKraken are driven by <code>tools/perf/apps_latency.py</code> over the DevTools protocol with real
    Chromium input events, timed inside the renderer from the event's own timestamp to the first animation frame whose
    diff shows the selected file (its path in the header, new rows in the body), so the clock stops at the same place:
    the first frame with the result on screen. "Select a file" clicks rows 3, 0 and 5 of the changes list; row 0 is the
    5,000-line file, which is why the Electron apps' click median hides a 2 to 4 second outlier in every round.
    GitKraken's file list cannot be walked with the keyboard, so it has no "next file" figure.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose my-8">
    <BenchmarkComparison client:visible />
  </div>

  <h2 id="budgets">Budgets</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Metric</th><th>Budget</th><th>Measured</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {BENCHMARK_METRICS.filter((m) => m.budget !== undefined).map((m) => (
        <tr>
          <td>{m.name}</td>
          <td>≤ {formatMetric(m.budget!, m.unit)}</td>
          <td><strong>{formatMetric(m.corvene, m.unit)}</strong></td>
        </tr>
      ))}
      <tr><td>Idle CPU</td><td>0 %</td><td>0.4–1.1 % focused, 0.0 % unfocused (still being chased)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="latency">Latency on a large repository</h2>
  <p>
    The actions people use most, on a synthetic worst case ({SYSTEM_SPECS.fixture}), before and after the optimisation
    pass of 2026-09-30. Both columns are Corvene; milliseconds are medians.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Action</th><th>Before (ms)</th><th>After (ms)</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {LATENCY_PASS.map((row) => (
        <tr>
          <td>{row.action}</td>
          <td>{row.before}</td>
          <td><strong>{row.after}</strong></td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    What changed: git work fans out on threads and reads come from gitoxide; diffs and changesets are cached by object id
    so a refresh that changed nothing draws nothing; views are cached and long lists virtualized; text shaping keeps its
    fonts between frames.
  </p>

  <h2 id="reproduce">Reproducing</h2>
  <p>The application comparison needs the release build and the two other apps installed:</p>
  <CodeBlock code={"cargo build --release -p corvene\npython3 tools/perf/apps.py --runs 5 --idle 60 --corvene-data <data dir with a repository added>"} />
  <p>The fixture generator and the latency bench live in <code>tools/perf</code> too. The bench speaks to a profiling build over <code>CORVENE_CONTROL</code>:</p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={"cargo build --profile profiling -p corvene --features snapshots\npython3 tools/perf/fixture.py big target/perf/big\npython3 tools/perf/bench.py target/perf/big"}
  />
  <p>
    Besides wall time the bench records the main thread's retired instructions per action, which does not move with
    machine load. The raw output of the application comparison is kept in <code>tools/perf/results/</code>.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
